# acts_good and regret_good: "good" could be read as outwardly good (good as an act done) or as good for deepening knowledge of good and evil.
# But P1, A1 and C all take these claims from the same sentence, S2, which uses "good" in the single sense the gnostic condition supplies, so I found no shift.
# A2 joins the claim about what nature cares for to the gnostic claim, which are different claims each in its own sense, so it is not a bridge.
